# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
# Copyright 2026 Cnext.eu
#
# =============================================================================
# Exemplar Domain Ontology — kairos-design-domain
# =============================================================================
#
# Purpose
# -------
# This file is a fully-commented, realistic example of a v5 domain ontology
# authored by the kairos-design-domain skill. Copy it as a starting point, then
# replace every class, property, and comment with your domain's content.
#
# What this exemplar demonstrates (and why):
#
#   1. @prefix : (the empty prefix) bound to the domain namespace as the *only*
#      self-namespace prefix (#446, #447 part 2).
#      Named prefixes (e.g. @prefix dcsa:) are for *external* namespaces only.
#      Declaring @prefix : here causes the compiler to treat this file as the
#      root ontology; any imported prefix that maps to a different namespace is
#      then flagged by safety.prefix-ambiguous, not your own declarations.
#      See kernel.py:_check_prefix_ambiguity — a prefix already present in
#      root_prefixes is skipped, so declaring @prefix : suppresses the warning.
#
#   2. File header block with domain scope, reused reference-model properties,
#      module precedence, and deliberate exclusions with reasons (#442).
#
#   3. Rich rdfs:comment with evidence references and naming/data cautions,
#      beyond Gate 5's mere presence check (#442).
#
#   4. skos:altLabel for business vocabulary — carry business aliases onto
#      canonical classes so discoverability tooling can find them (#442).
#
#   5. Object properties with declared ranges — relationships are first-class
#      canonical citizens, not an afterthought (#442).
#
#   6. One local owl:Class subclass where the hub constrains a reference-model
#      class — the right time to subclass, and when not to (#442).
#
#   7. _foundation.ttl — this hub's optional shared-conventions ontology is
#      scaffolded but inert by default (opt-in). See the comment on the
#      owl:imports line below (#447 part 1).
#
# Paired SHACL governance shape:
#   See exemplar-domain.shacl.ttl for a hand-authored governance constraint
#   with sh:message and explicit severity (#443).
#
# =============================================================================
# Domain scope
# =============================================================================
# Domain: events
# Hub archetype: unit-load-carrier (logistics accelerator)
#
# Canonical scope: the events domain owns the canonical vocabulary for
# transport-event reporting — the *what happened, when, where* — as distinct
# from the party, equipment, and document domains.
#
# =============================================================================
# Reused reference-model properties
# =============================================================================
# This domain imports terms from two managed reference-model modules:
#   - dcsa/events      (DCSA event vocabulary: TransportEvent, EventLocation)
#   - dcsa/track-and-trace (DCSA tracking vocabulary: TrackingEvent)
# Both modules are mandated for the events domain by data-domains.yaml.
# Where a property already exists in a reference model, *reuse* it via
# owl:imports rather than re-declaring a copy.
#
# Module precedence: dcsa/events and dcsa/track-and-trace both publish the
# same class IRIs for the core event vocabulary. This is an upstream
# reference-model defect (see kairos-ontology-referencemodels issue for the
# dcsa/events overlap). The toolkit downgrades term_owner_ambiguous to a
# warning when all candidate modules are required imports, so this hub
# validates cleanly with a warning.
#
# =============================================================================
# Deliberate exclusions
# =============================================================================
# Excluded from this slice (with reasons):
#   - VesselPositionEvent: the vessel domain owns positional telemetry;
#     including it here would create a grain collision (see pattern library
#     grain_collisions for the party/equipment boundary).
#   - DocumentEvent: the document domain owns the document lifecycle;
#     a DocumentEvent is a fact *about* a document, not a transport event.
#   - BookingConfirmation: a business process, not a transport event;
#     belongs in a future booking domain.
# =============================================================================

@prefix : <https://example.test/ont/events#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix dcsa: <https://referencemodels.kairos-ontology.eu/dcsa/events#> .
@prefix dcsa-tnt: <https://referencemodels.kairos-ontology.eu/dcsa/track-and-trace#> .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ontology declaration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ontology IRI MUST match the catalog-v001.xml entry that init --domain
# registers. Use resolve-ontology <domain> --json-output to read existing IRIs;
# never invent one.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

<https://example.test/ont/events> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Transport Events Domain"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Domain ontology for transport-event reporting under the
    unit-load-carrier archetype. Owns the canonical vocabulary for
    what-happened-when-where in the transport lifecycle, distinct from party,
    equipment, and document domains.

    Evidence: DCSA JIT/transparency standard v3.1 (section 4.2);
    source: carrier TMS export 'event_log' table (PII-safe samples confirmed
    in kairos-design-discovery).

    Naming caution: 'event' is overloaded across source systems — a
    'gate-in' in the TMS is a transport event, but a 'login event' in the
    IAM system is not. This domain covers transport events only.
    """@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.2.0" ;

    # _foundation.ttl is scaffolded by init but inert by default (opt-in).
    # Uncomment the import below (and the catalog-v001.xml <uri> entry) to
    # share base conventions across domains. See #447 part 1.
    # owl:imports <https://example.test/ont/_foundation> ;

    # Reference-model imports — mandated by data-domains.yaml for the events
    # domain. Both modules publish overlapping class IRIs; the toolkit emits
    # a term_owner_ambiguous warning (not an error) because both are required.
    owl:imports <https://referencemodels.kairos-ontology.eu/dcsa/events> ;
    owl:imports <https://referencemodels.kairos-ontology.eu/dcsa/track-and-trace> .

# =============================================================================
# Classes
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TransportEvent — primary canonical class
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reuses dcsa:TransportEvent from the reference model (imported above).
# We do NOT subclass it — the hub merely *uses* this class. A local subclass
# is only warranted when the hub adds a constraint or property the reference
# model does not carry (see LocalTransportEvent below).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

dcsa:TransportEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Transport Event"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Event"@en, "Tracking Event"@en, "Transport Event Log Entry"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A discrete occurrence in the transport lifecycle: a gate-in,
    gate-out, loading, discharge, or arrival event, sourced from the carrier
    TMS 'event_log' table.

    Evidence: DCSA JIT standard §4.2; source column 'event_type_cd' carries
    the enumerated codes {GATE_IN, GATE_OUT, LOAD, DISCHARGE, ARRIVAL}.

    Data caution: source 'event_timestamp' is in carrier local time without
    timezone offset; the binding must normalise to UTC. See EntityBinding
    expression for the timezone cast.
    """@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://dcsa.org/standards/jit> .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LocalTransportEvent — local subclass
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is a local owl:Class that constrains a reference-model class. The hub
# adds the 'eventConfidence' property which dcsa:TransportEvent does not carry.
# Subclassing is the correct move here because:
#   - the hub ADDS a property to the reference-model class, and
#   - grain_collisions does NOT name dcsa:TransportEvent.
# If grain_collisions named it, subclassing would be forbidden (the
# do-not-subclass-and-do-not-merge boundary). If the hub merely used the class
# without adding anything, no subclass would be needed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

:LocalTransportEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf dcsa:TransportEvent ;
    rdfs:label "Local Transport Event"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Carrier Event"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A TransportEvent enriched with carrier-specific confidence
    scoring. This subclass exists because the hub adds the eventConfidence
    property, which the DCSA reference model does not carry. It is NOT a
    generalisation of all transport events — those use dcsa:TransportEvent
    directly. See the kairos-design-domain pattern library section on local
    subclasses for when this is and is not appropriate.
    """@en .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EventLocation — reused from reference model, no subclass
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hub uses this class as the range of the hasLocation property below.
# It does NOT add any properties or constraints, so no local subclass is
# needed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

dcsa:EventLocation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Event Location"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Location"@en, "Event Place"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The geographic or facility location where a transport event occurred."@en .

# =============================================================================
# Datatype properties
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# eventConfidence — local property on the local subclass
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

:eventConfidence a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "event confidence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Carrier-specific confidence score (0.0–1.0) for the event,
    derived from the TMS 'event_confidence_pct' column divided by 100.
    Only present on :LocalTransportEvent, not on the base dcsa:TransportEvent.
    """@en ;
    rdfs:domain :LocalTransportEvent ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal .

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# eventTimestamp — reused from reference model
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not re-declared — dcsa:eventTimestamp is imported via owl:imports above.
# The binding maps source 'event_timestamp' to dcsa:eventTimestamp with a
# timezone normalisation expression. See kairos-design-mapping.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# eventCode — reused from reference model
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not re-declared — dcsa:eventCode is imported via owl:imports above.

# =============================================================================
# Object properties (relationships)
# =============================================================================
# cldn20 declared zero object properties. This exemplar shows that
# relationships are first-class canonical citizens with declared ranges,
# not an afterthought. Declare the range even when you are not 100% certain
# of the target — an omitted range compiles; a wrong range does not.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

:hasLocation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has location"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The location where a transport event occurred."@en ;
    rdfs:domain dcsa:TransportEvent ;
    rdfs:range dcsa:EventLocation .

:involvesEquipment a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "involves equipment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The equipment (container, chassis) involved in a transport
    event. Range is an external class from the equipment domain; declare the
    IRI even before that domain is authored — see the pattern library's
    deferred-relationship guidance.
    """@en ;
    rdfs:domain dcsa:TransportEvent ;
    rdfs:range <https://referencemodels.kairos-ontology.eu/dcsa/equipment#Equipment> .

:nextEvent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "next event"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The chronologically next transport event in the lifecycle, if any."@en ;
    rdfs:domain dcsa:TransportEvent ;
    rdfs:range dcsa:TransportEvent .
